XVII. The “Extended Republic” Argument

One of the most interesting American Federalist arguments was that a larger republic could actually make domination by a single faction more difficult.

Madison argued that increasing the size and diversity of the republic could multiply competing interests and thereby make majority oppression more difficult. (National Archives)

Neo Pan-Middle-Easternism could adapt this argument.

A federation encompassing many communities would contain numerous political interests.

Instead of organizing politics around one binary conflict, federal politics could contain competing coalitions concerning:

* economics
* religion
* geography
* language
* urbanization
* environmental policy
* energy
* trade
* social policy
* regional development

The constitutional challenge would be to transform diversity from a source of instability into a system of representation.

⸻

XVIII. Jerusalem as a Federal Question

A Neo Pan-Middle-Eastern federation would inevitably confront Jerusalem.

Rather than treating Jerusalem exclusively as the possession of one national government, a federal constitutional framework could potentially treat the city as a special federal territory or jointly administered federal district.

Its administration could guarantee access and protection for the city’s religious and cultural communities.

Such an arrangement would not automatically resolve the underlying political disputes, but federal institutions could provide mechanisms through which those disputes are constitutionally negotiated.

